title secp256k1 ECDSA verify in Rye, parity-GREEN
prompt take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first; Rye prioritized, Glow on green-witnessed Rye; send often; stop at custody gates
think Read REMEMBER head, the 1024-round itinerary, the six-season double-seat, and the decision wave — Season G (Cryptography) is the highest-Lindy active thread, and the git log shows the secp256k1 tower climbing rung by rung (base field, group law, scalarmul, scalar field mod n).
think The docs and every secp256k1 header name the same next crux: ECDSA verification — the last composition over the four GREEN rungs, unblocking Kumara identity against a Bitcoin/Ethereum key and every module that must CHECK a secp256k1 signature. Verification is the non-gated half; signing stays the custody gate.
think Studied the scalar (little-endian reduce/mul/invert/is_canonical), scalarmul (double-and-add ladder), group (complete RCB add/dbl/affine_x), and base field (from_bytes/to_bytes big-endian, square/mul/add/invert) APIs, plus Zig's std.crypto.sign.ecdsa verifyPrehashed for exact parity of the hash-to-scalar reduction.
think Authored crypto/secp256k1_ecdsa.rye: SEC 1 verify — range gate [1,n-1], hash mod n, w = s^-1, u1 = z·w, u2 = r·w, R = u1·G + u2·Q, refuse infinity, accept iff affine_x(R) mod n == r. Base point G is the SEC 2 generator parsed through the proven field, never raw limbs. Added an on-curve check (y²=x³+7) beyond the reference's verify path, since it takes affine coords directly.
obs Two build reds, both mechanical: u1/u2 shadow Zig primitive type names (renamed u1_le/u2_le), and I initially pasted n's limbs in the malleability test helper — replaced with the reference's own scalar.neg so no order-n pattern is pasted anywhere, honoring the same discipline the scalar field keeps for n.
obs Parity: our verdict equals Zig's EcdsaSecp256k1Sha256 true-for-true and false-for-false across 8 deterministic key pairs × 5 messages, plus algebraic tampers (r, s, hash, coordinate), out-of-range zero r/s refused, off-curve key refused, and the (r, n−s) malleability fact holds.
obs crypto/README.md is stale independent of this round — it predates all four earlier secp256k1 rungs (wrong "thirty files" count, no secp256k1 section). Left for its own sync round; docs/CRYPTO.md is the current compressed front door and carries the correct thirty-seven-file count and the new ECDSA row.
